  {
    "episode_id": 76922,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76922.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "I think if you had said to me a year and a half ago, hey, Jake, you're going to be a member of Congress giving a speech on the House floor to impeach the president for inciting insurrection in the middle of the pandemic. I would have thought that all of those things were implausible. So it's certainly been quite a 2020. It was a somber and momentous occasion. I took no joy in it. It was really very sad in a lot of ways. But on Sunday, January 3, I took an oath to support and defend the Constitution. And before I walked into the house chamber, I spent a long time with my dad looking at that huge portrait of the signing of the Constitution that hangs above the house gallery. It's got Franklin and Washington and others just really kind of contemplating the brilliance of this American experiment, the resilience of our constitutional system designed to make ambition counteracting ambition, as Madison said. And then to see on Wednesday, 72 hours later, that it can be also very fragile, and that it requires defending. It was stark. But I swore an oath to defend the Constitution against all enemies born in domestic. And it's an oath you got to take seriously. And it's one that does not have any asterisks about it for unless the president will tweet something mean about me. No, it's an ironclad.",
