  {
    "episode_id": 76922,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76922.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "So I first got elected to the city council by knocking doors. I did nothing complicated except every day when I out for four and a half hours and knocked on people's doors and had conversations. So it was at a personal level, very disappointing to me to not be able to establish that raw connection. And the danger of that is that you start to over-index on social media, right? Because every political campaign is so hungry for feedback loops about how you're doing. And at the congressional level, you're not doing polling very frequently. You do maybe one or two polls. So you don't have daily or weekly or even monthly feedback on the state of the race. All you can see is fundraising numbers, which are only a very, very rough proxy. And you get some chatter. So people look for indicators on social media That's a not a good idea because Twitter is not representative of the electorate. Facebook is not representative of the electorate, at least the comments are not. And I was constantly reminding my team, constantly reminding my team, we need to be very careful. We need to not freak out about sort of the daily Twitter thread. We need to focus on what voters in Taunton and Attaboro are actually going to be concerned about. because that was number one. Number two was, it was interesting sort of arbitrage opportunities that it creates political campaigns in Congress are very predicated on TV spending, on broadcast and cable TV. And it was a period in March, April and May when my TV consultants came to me and they said, you know, something really weird is happening. Everybody is locked up inside and they are really hungry for news about what's happening. We're seeing TV viewership go up to about eight hours a day on average, which is mind blowing. It's like two or three acts, the typical median. Now, normally that would mean that advertising costs would soar too, because you pay an advertising cost per point, you know, per the percentage of the population that you reach. And so they're, they're efficient. But advertisers were pulling back because they were facing down the worst recession in a hundred years when it gets shot. Right. So he was like, you can reach 50 times more, not 50, three or four times more people for half the cost if you put up advertising right now in April or May. But that's way too early by normal political judgment to advertise. I mean, that's very early in a campaign to go up on TV. And so we had this debate that no political consultant or campaign it had in 30 years about do you take advantage of this opportunity? We decided ultimately we decided not to, which I think was the right decision.",
